Nine stages. This bookʼs map of the Singularity, from Stage 1, the Cash
Grab, to Stage 9, the Apex Intelligence. Stages 1 to 5 organize its near-term
social choices; the whole sequence remains a proposed map. (Chapters 2
to 4, Appendix C)
Open weights. A model whose trained parameters are available to down-
load, subject to its license and access terms. Downloaded copies can run
locally on suitable hardware, but availability alone doesnʼt grant unre-
stricted rights or provide the training data. (Chapter 2)
Photobioreactor. A  vessel  that  grows  algae  under  controlled  light  and
conditions. The builder in Chapter 17 printed one at home. (Chapter 17)
Precedent Ledger. The bookʼs twenty-four documented cases of people
meeting a new capability, or a new rule, before this one, each with a
story, a mechanism, a rule, and a practice. (Introduction and Appendix D)
Premortem. Imagining that a plan has already failed and working back-
ward to find out why, before it fails for real. (Chapter 18)
Primate Backlash. Stage 5: grief and anger at the change, including real
objections, sometimes expressed with a brick. (Chapter 2)
Recursive self-improvement. An AI system helping to design the next,
better version of itself. (Chapters 1 and 3)
SB 1383. Californiaʼs law requiring large food businesses to recover edible
food and setting a target to recover 20 percent of edible food that would
otherwise be landfilled. (Chapters 14 and 19)
Short loop. A tighter circle of food, repair, tools, and knowledge drawn
around  your  region,  so  it  keeps  circulating  when  the  long  global  line
hiccups. (Chapter 14)
Shouse. A shop-house: half workshop, half compact home, under one
roof. (Chapter 13)
Singularity (this bookʼs definition). The point where intelligence stops
being  scarce,  and  every  institution  quietly  built  on  its  scarcity  starts
failing before anyone has written the replacement. (Chapter 1)
Sneakernet. Moving data by carrying it: a memory card handed person to
person when the network isnʼt there. (Chapter 16)
